{"maxId":424,"entries":{"chapter-61-study-parkinson-disease-and-related-disorders|Chapter 61 Study: Parkinson Disease and Related Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Parkinsonism = 6 core motor features: bradykinesia (central feature, from SNpc dopaminergic loss), rest tremor, rigidity, loss of postural reflexes, flexed posture, gait freezing (p935).":["u3"],"copy|PD is the most common neurodegenerative cause of parkinsonism, affecting 1% to 2% of people over age 60 (p935).":["u4"],"copy|Diagnostic gold standard = autopsy Lewy bodies in the SNpc; 10% to 20% of clinically diagnosed PD is wrong on autopsy, and ~20% of older adults without known parkinsonism have midbrain Lewy bodies at autopsy (p936).":["u5"],"copy|Incidence: 4.5–19/100,000 person-years across all ages; 27.2–107.2/100,000 person-years for age >60 (p936).":["u6"],"copy|Prevalence: 100–200/100,000 overall; >1 in 100 for age >60; PD is about 1.5× more common in men (p936).":["u7"],"copy|>90% of PD is sporadic; 20 known PARK genetic loci (p937). GBA (GBA1) mutations are linked to early-onset PD, particularly in patients of Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry (p938).":["u8"],"copy|Atypical parkinsonisms (PSP, MSA, CBS) have a prevalence of roughly 5% to 10% of PD's (p938).":["u9"],"copy|Normal aging: lose 0.5% to 1% of nigrostriatal nerve terminals per year after the 3rd decade. By PD motor-symptom onset, 60% to 80% of posterior-putamen dopaminergic terminals are already lost (p938).":["u10"],"copy|Direct pathway (D1, excitatory, pro-movement) vs indirect pathway (D2, inhibitory, anti-movement); dopamine loss shifts balance toward the indirect pathway → net hypokinesia (p938).":["u11"],"copy|Braak staging (6 stages total): stages 1–2 = premotor (olfactory, sleep, autonomic); stage 3 = nigral involvement = onset of motor symptoms; stages 5–6 = cortical Lewy bodies = cognitive decline (p939).":["u12"],"copy|PDD vs DLB \"1-year rule\": motor symptoms precede cognitive symptoms by >1 year in PDD; motor and cognitive symptoms arise within the same year in DLB (p939).":["u13"],"copy|Levodopa responds best for bradykinesia + rigidity; axial features (hypophonia, dysphagia, postural instability) are often medication-refractory (p939). ⟨2026 exam Q15⟩":["u14"],"copy|Alternative-diagnosis clues: symmetric onset, poor levodopa response, absent rest tremor (p939).":["u15"],"copy|2015 MDS criteria (\"clinically established\"): no exclusionary criteria + bradykinesia + ≥2 of 4 supportive criteria (levodopa response, levodopa-induced dyskinesias, rest tremor, olfactory loss and/or cardiac sympathetic denervation) (p940). ⟨2026 exam Q31⟩":["u16"],"copy|DaT SPECT confirms nigrostriatal dopaminergic loss but cannot distinguish PD from PSP/MSA/DLB — all show loss; it is approved to distinguish essential tremor from PD (atypical tremor) and can also help distinguish PD from drug-induced parkinsonism (p940–941). ⟨2026 exam Q60⟩":["u17"],"copy|PSP key features: supranuclear gaze palsy / loss of downward ocular movements, with falls early in the course; CBS: asymmetric rigidity/dystonia with limb myoclonus, alien-limb phenomenon, and cortical sensory loss (Table 61-3 p940, Table 61-4 p941).":["u18"],"copy|MSA red flags: severe dysautonomia, cerebellar signs, nocturnal stridor; median MSA onset is in the 6th decade, mean time to severe disability ~5 years from diagnosis (p942–943).":["u19"],"copy|~50% of PD patients develop dementia (PDD) within 10 years of diagnosis (p942).":["u20"],"copy|Levodopa/carbidopa (Sinemet) or levodopa/benserazide (Madopar): most effective drug; >50% develop wearing-off/dyskinesias after 5 years of therapy (p943).":["u21"],"copy|Entacapone (COMT-I) — 200 mg with each levodopa dose; ↑ dyskinesias, may need lower levodopa dose (p944).":["u22"],"copy|Tolcapone — 100–200 mg tablets; hepatotoxicity risk → removed from US market (p944).":["u23"],"copy|Pramipexole/ropinirole (dopamine agonists) — hallucinations/psychosis more likely in older adults; safer to use levodopa in patients >70 years (p944).":["u24"],"copy|Ergot agonists (pergolide, bromocriptine, cabergoline) — fibrosis risk (cardiac valvulopathy, retroperitoneal/pleuropulmonary/pericardial); pergolide withdrawn from the US market (p944).":["u25"],"copy|Rotigotine (transdermal patch) — for swallowing difficulty / nocturnal akinesia (p944).":["u26"],"copy|Apomorphine (subcut) / sublingual film (Kynmobi) — may be the most powerful agonist; used for rapid \"off\" rescue; intense nausea (p944).":["u27"],"copy|Amantadine — usually 100 mg BID for PD symptoms, usually 300–400 mg/day for antidyskinetic effect; confusion, hallucinations, livedo reticularis; often poorly tolerated in older adults (p944). Only established effective antidyskinetic agent (antiglutamatergic).":["u28"],"copy|Table 61-5 also lists domperidone (peripheral dopamine-receptor blocker for GI upset from levodopa/agonists; not available in the US) and MAO-B inhibitors (selegiline, rasagiline, safinamide) — mild symptomatic benefit, no proven neuroprotective benefit (p944).":["u29"],"copy|Opicapone (COMT-I) — once-daily dosing; istradefylline (A2A antagonist) — once-daily, reduces off-time with carbidopa/levodopa in patients with motor fluctuations (p945).":["u30"],"copy|Wearing-off = adequate levodopa dose fails to last ≥6 hours (p944); more frequent levodopa dosing may be the simplest approach; MAO-B inhibitors help mild wearing-off slightly, and COMT inhibitors are useful (p945). ⟨2026 exam Q83⟩":["u31"],"copy|Peak-dose dyskinesia: reduce levodopa dose, or add amantadine (start 100 mg BID, up to 200 mg BID); buspirone up to 20 mg/day may benefit some patients (p946).":["u32"],"copy|Diphasic dyskinesias (Table 61-6, p945; p946) — occur at the start/end of dose (not peak), mixed chorea/dystonia, mainly affect the legs; distinct from peak-dose dyskinesia above.":["u33"],"copy|Drug-induced hallucinations: reduce dopaminergic burden first; then quetiapine (start 25 mg qhs) or pimavanserin (17 mg 1–2×/day, reduce with CYP3A4 inhibitors) (p946). ⟨2026 exam Q75⟩Current practice (not the book): pimavanserin 34 mg PO once daily, no titration; 10 mg once daily with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors — FDA Nuplazid label, 2026.":["u34"],"copy|Clozapine more effective but agranulocytosis in 1% to 2% → weekly CBC; try quetiapine/pimavanserin first (p946–947).":["u35"],"copy|Severe psychosis needing drug withdrawal: taper anti-PD meds stepwise over 3 days — abrupt levodopa withdrawal risks a neuroleptic-malignant-like syndrome (p946).":["u36"],"copy|RBD: clonazepam 0.5–2 mg at bedtime; melatonin as a less-effective first try (p947).":["u37"],"copy|Antimuscarinics (trihexyphenidyl, benztropine) — avoid in patients >70 years: cognitive impairment, hallucinations, and linked to freezing of gait (p947).":["u38"],"copy|DBS (STN or GPi target) — FDA-approved 2002; for motor fluctuations/dyskinesias or medication-refractory tremor; does not improve postural instability or other axial features (p949); Current practice (not the book): NICE recommends subcutaneous foslevodopa/foscarbidopa infusion as an option for adults with advanced levodopa-responsive Parkinson disease, severe motor fluctuations and hyperkinesia or dyskinesia despite available medicines, only when apomorphine or DBS cannot be used or no longer controls symptoms and the commercial arrangement applies; FDA approved it on 16 October 2024 for motor fluctuations in adults with advanced Parkinson disease - NICE TA934 2023, 1.1 p4; FDA NDA216962 approval package cover and approval letter p1":["u39"],"copy|Focused ultrasound (FUS) — FDA-approved 2018 for tremor; ablative/non-programmable, no anesthesia needed (p949).":["u40"],"copy|Table 61-7 lists antimuscarinics for tremor with no caveat, but the text on p947 says avoid them in patients over 70 (p946–947).":["u41"],"copy|Table 61-7's psychosis row (p947) lists only clozapine and quetiapine; the text on p946 adds pimavanserin, which unlike quetiapine has prospective trial data for PD psychosis (p946–947).":["u42"],"recall|Recall":["u44"],"2026-exam-questions-for-this-chapter|2026 exam questions for this chapter":["u45"],"copy|א. Tremor and dysphagia":["u46"],"copy|ב. Tremor and autonomic dysfunction":["u47"],"copy|ג. Bradykinesia and limb rigidity ← key":["u48"],"copy|ד. Bradykinesia and hypophonia":["u49"],"copy|א. Dystonia developing on low-dose levodopa":["u50"],"copy|ב. Freezing of gait and orthostatic hypotension as initial symptoms":["u51"],"copy|ג. Dementia developing a year from the onset of motor symptoms":["u52"],"copy|ד. Onset of asymmetric rest tremor of the hands ← key":["u53"],"copy|א. Presence of tremor":["u54"],"copy|ב. Severe, early autonomic dysfunction ← key":["u55"],"copy|ג. Asymmetric onset":["u56"],"copy|ד. Visual hallucinations":["u57"],"copy|א. Start haloperidol":["u58"],"copy|ב. Start entacapone":["u59"],"copy|ג. Reduce the dopaminergic burden ← key":["u60"],"copy|ד. Stop all antiparkinsonian drugs immediately":["u61"],"copy|א. Increase the frequency of levodopa dosing or add a COMT inhibitor ← key":["u62"],"copy|ב. Stop carbidopa/levodopa and switch to dopamine-agonist monotherapy":["u63"],"copy|ג. Quetiapine to reduce the fluctuations":["u64"],"copy|ד. Start deep brain stimulation early":["u65"],"copy|CHAPTER 61s / HAZZARD":["u66"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 61 — Parkinson Disease and Related Disorders; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u67"],"copy|Definition & epidemiology":["u68"],"copy|Pathophysiology":["u69"],"copy|Diagnosis & distinguishing PD from atypical parkinsonism":["u70"],"copy|Management — dopaminergic drugs":["u71"],"copy|Management — dyskinesias & nonmotor complications":["u72"],"copy|Surgical therapy":["u73"],"copy|1.":["u75"],"copy|What six motor features define parkinsonism, and which one is considered central?":["u76"],"copy|Bradykinesia, rest tremor, rigidity, loss of postural reflexes, flexed posture, and gait freezing; bradykinesia is the central feature (p935).":["u77"],"copy|4.":["u78"],"copy|What fraction of PD cases is sporadic, and how many PARK genetic loci are recognized?":["u79"],"copy|Over 90% sporadic; 20 PARK loci (p937).":["u80"],"copy|8.":["u81"],"copy|What is the \"1-year rule\" used to distinguish PDD from DLB?":["u82"],"copy|In PDD motor symptoms precede cognitive symptoms by more than 1 year; in DLB, motor and cognitive symptoms arise within the same year (p939).":["u83"],"copy|10.":["u84"],"copy|Can DaT SPECT distinguish PD from PSP, MSA, or DLB?":["u85"],"copy|No — all of these show nigrostriatal dopaminergic loss on DaT SPECT (p940–941).":["u86"],"copy|It is approved to distinguish essential tremor from PD in atypical tremor, and can help distinguish PD from drug-induced parkinsonism (p941).":["u87"],"copy|11.":["u88"],"copy|Which ergot dopamine agonists carry a fibrosis risk, and which one has been withdrawn from the US market?":["u89"],"copy|Pergolide, bromocriptine, and cabergoline carry fibrosis risk (cardiac valvulopathy, retroperitoneal/pleuropulmonary/pericardial fibrosis); pergolide has been withdrawn from the US market (p944).":["u90"],"copy|12.":["u91"],"copy|Above what age is levodopa generally preferred over dopamine agonists, and why?":["u92"],"copy|Above age 70, because dopamine agonists are more likely than levodopa to cause hallucinations, confusion, and psychosis in older adults (p944).":["u93"],"copy|13.":["u94"],"copy|How is \"wearing-off\" defined, and what is the simplest first-line management step?":["u95"],"copy|An adequate levodopa dose that fails to last at least 6 hours before parkinsonian symptoms return (p944); more frequent levodopa dosing may be the simplest approach; an MAO-B inhibitor can help mild wearing-off slightly and COMT inhibitors are useful (p945).":["u96"],"copy|14.":["u97"],"copy|What is the only established effective antidyskinetic agent, and what receptor does it block?":["u98"],"copy|Amantadine, via blockade of glutamate NMDA receptors (antiglutamatergic action) (p944).":["u99"],"copy|15.":["u100"],"copy|For drug-induced psychosis in PD, if anti-PD medications must be withdrawn, how should this be done and why?":["u101"],"copy|Stepwise, over about 3 days — sudden levodopa withdrawal risks a neuroleptic-malignant-like syndrome (p946).":["u102"],"copy|Q15 — sourced to p.":["u104"],"copy|939.":["u105"],"copy|Which combination of symptoms is expected to respond best to levodopa in a patient with Parkinson disease?":["u106"],"copy|Book: p939: the features that tend to improve the most with levodopa or dopamine agonists include bradykinesia and rigidity in the arms and legs; axial features including hypophonia and dysphagia are often medication refractory.":["u107"],"copy|Q31 — sourced to p.":["u108"],"copy|939–940.":["u109"],"copy|Which clinical pattern supports Parkinson disease and not parkinsonism?":["u110"],"copy|Book: Table 61-4 (p941): PD inclusion criteria include rest tremor and asymmetric onset (++) (ד); DLB inclusion includes dementia within 1 y of the onset of motor features (ג); PD exclusion includes freezing of gait developing within 3 y of motor onset (–) (the freezing half of ב).":["u111"],"copy|Table 61-3 (p940): dystonia induced with low-dose levodopa → MSA (א); pronounced postural hypotension → MSA (the other half of ב). p939: PD often begins asymmetrically.":["u112"],"copy|Q60 — sourced to p.":["u113"],"copy|941.":["u114"],"copy|A 68-year-old man with parkinsonism presents with severe orthostatic hypotension, urinary incontinence, erectile dysfunction and cerebellar ataxia; cognition is preserved.":["u115"],"copy|Which finding most supports multiple system atrophy over Parkinson disease?":["u116"],"copy|Book: Table 61-4 (p941): MSA inclusion, prominent dysautonomia (+++); PD exclusion, severe dysautonomia unrelated to medications; hallucinations are an MSA exclusion (disposes of ד). p943: features of MSA that differ from PD include severe autonomic symptoms (orthostatic light-headedness, blood pressure fluctuations, urinary dysfunction).":["u117"],"copy|Q75 — sourced to p.":["u118"],"copy|946.":["u119"],"copy|An 80-year-old man with Parkinson disease on a dopamine agonist develops prominent visual hallucinations and confusion.":["u120"],"copy|Initial work-up excluded infection, a metabolic disturbance and an acute finding on brain CT.":["u121"],"copy|What is the most appropriate strategy?":["u122"],"copy|Book: p946: consider reducing the total dose of dopaminergic medication to whatever degree is tolerable; if drugs are withdrawn, do it stepwise over 3 days, because sudden levodopa withdrawal can cause a neuroleptic malignant-like syndrome. p947: other antipsychotics, typical or atypical, \"are more likely to worsen the parkinsonism; therefore, they should be avoided\" (disposes of א).":["u123"],"copy|Q83 — sourced to p.":["u124"],"copy|945.":["u125"],"copy|A 69-year-old woman with Parkinson disease on immediate-release carbidopa/levodopa every 4 hours reports worsening bradykinesia and rigidity about 30–45 minutes before each dose; a clear \"off\" state is seen on examination.":["u126"],"copy|Which strategy is recommended?":["u127"],"copy|Book: p945 (wearing-off): more frequent dosing of levodopa may be the simplest approach; COMT inhibitors have been found useful for treating wearing-off.":["u128"],"copy|Answer":["u129","u130","u131","u132","u133","u134","u135","u136","u137"],"what-the-exams-asked|What the exams asked":["u138"],"copy|Recognizing idiopathic Parkinson disease and distinguishing rest from action tremor June 2023 Q80, June 2026 Q31, May 2024 Q50, September 2024 Q24, December 2021 Q92.":["u139"],"copy|What levodopa improves and what a transporter scan can establish June 2026 Q15, June 2025 Q64.":["u140"],"copy|Early autonomic failure with ataxia suggests multiple system atrophy June 2026 Q60.":["u141"],"copy|Nigral cell loss, exposure associations and the relative frequency of atypical syndromes May 2024 Q37, September 2024 Q59, September 2024 Q74.":["u142"],"copy|Initial levodopa, motor fluctuations and the different adverse effects of agonists May 2024 Q61, June 2025 Q11, September 2024 Q76, June 2022 Q89.":["u143"],"copy|Predictable end-of-dose immobility versus involuntary movements at peak dose June 2026 Q83, September 2024 Q86, June 2022 Q45.":["u144"],"copy|Hallucinations from dopamine therapy versus depression accompanying the disease June 2026 Q75, May 2024 Q95, December 2021 Q31.":["u145"],"copy|Nonmotor constipation and dream enactment during REM sleep June 2022 Q17, September 2024 Q81.":["u146"],"copy|Selecting patients for deep brain stimulation June 2025 Q82, December 2021 Q4.":["u147"],"copy|Presyncope and a dementia drug: the Basic-track reference names Chapter 59 but prints Parkinson treatment pages June 2026 Basic Q102.":["u148"],"reading-the-movement-examination|Reading the movement examination":["u149"],"why-the-motor-syndrome-develops|Why the motor syndrome develops":["u150"],"when-to-alter-dopaminergic-treatment|When to alter dopaminergic treatment":["u151"],"sleep-cognition-and-function|Sleep, cognition and function":["u152"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u153"],"copy|PDD: dementia begins more than one year after established motor parkinsonism. DLB: dementia begins before parkinsonism, together with it, or within one year after motor onset (p939, DLB Consortium 2017).":["u13"],"copy|Amantadine, immediate release. The chapter describes 100 mg twice daily for motor symptoms and higher doses for dyskinesia (p944). In older adults, reduced renal clearance may cause accumulation. Use renal-function-adjusted dosing and monitor confusion, hallucinations, edema and livedo reticularis. The chapter's 300–400 mg/day range is not a routine geriatric starting dose (US prescribing information).":["u28"],"copy|For peak-dose dyskinesia, consider smaller levodopa doses or amantadine when appropriate. The chapter describes 100–200 mg twice daily in selected patients (p946). For immediate-release amantadine, tailor initiation and escalation to renal function, age and neuropsychiatric tolerability. The text also describes buspirone up to 20 mg/day in selected patients.":["u32"],"copy|For persistent PD psychosis the chapter considers clozapine after quetiapine or pimavanserin, citing agranulocytosis in 1–2% (p946–947). Follow the absolute neutrophil count: with stable counts, weekly during the first 6 months, every 2 weeks during months 7–12, then monthly (US clozapine labeling).":["u35"],"copy|Focused ultrasound creates an irreversible, nonprogrammable lesion. The 2018 US indication was unilateral thalamotomy for medication-refractory tremor-dominant PD. Patients are generally awake, and local anesthetic can be used to secure the stereotactic frame (p949).":["u40"],"copy|Bradykinesia is the central motor feature.":["u154"],"copy|Look for a gradual, often asymmetric onset and tremor in a supported limb at rest.":["u155"],"copy|Tremor on holding the arms out or when writing points toward essential tremor, although mixed tremor and coexistence are possible.":["u156"],"copy|The December 2021 patient spilled soup from a spoon, had bilateral hand and head tremor, and improved after alcohol.":["u157"],"copy|The official treatment choice was a beta blocker.":["u158"],"copy|Hazzard explains the clinical distinction; propranolol is specifically supported for limb tremor by the AAN essential-tremor guideline (p940; AAN clinician summary, 2011).":["u159"],"copy|A supportive sign is different from a prerequisite.":["u160"],"copy|For clinically established PD the chapter describes bradykinesia, no exclusion criterion and at least two supportive findings among levodopa response, levodopa-induced dyskinesia, rest tremor, and olfactory loss or cardiac sympathetic denervation.":["u161"],"copy|September 2024 selected rest tremor.":["u162"],"copy|June 2026 selected tremor beginning asymmetrically at rest.":["u163"],"copy|June 2023 paired bradykinesia with impaired postural reflexes.":["u164"],"copy|Prominent early postural instability raises suspicion of an alternative disorder, even though impairment can appear later in PD (p939-941, Tables 61-3 and 61-4).":["u165"],"copy|Limb versus axial response.":["u166"],"copy|Levodopa usually helps distal dexterity and limb rigidity most.":["u167"],"copy|Tremor responds variably.":["u168"],"copy|Hypophonia, dysphagia and postural instability often reflect additional nondopaminergic injury.":["u169"],"copy|In June 2026 this distinction separated the limb pair from mixed pairs that contained an axial or autonomic complaint (p939).":["u170"],"copy|DaT SPECT establishes terminal loss.":["u171"],"copy|Essential tremor may look like PD but normally preserves striatal uptake.":["u172"],"copy|PSP, DLB and MSA can each reduce uptake.":["u173"],"copy|Thus an abnormal scan supports degenerative parkinsonism without naming the underlying disease.":["u174"],"copy|Early severe orthostatic hypotension, bladder or erectile dysfunction together with cerebellar ataxia favor MSA.":["u175"],"copy|Downward gaze palsy and early backward falls favor PSP.":["u176"],"copy|Asymmetric dystonia with apraxia, cortical sensory loss or an alien limb suggests corticobasal syndrome (p939-943).":["u177"],"copy|The characteristic lesions are misfolded alpha-synuclein in Lewy bodies and loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta.":["u178"],"copy|Losing nigrostriatal input favors inhibition of movement.":["u179"],"copy|In contrast to the milder terminal loss of ordinary aging, the posterior and dorsal putamen are preferentially affected.":["u180"],"copy|An estimated 60% to 80% of posterior-putaminal terminals have already been lost by motor onset.":["u181"],"copy|The Braak model places symptomatic nigral involvement at stage 3 and allows sleep, autonomic and olfactory changes to precede it (p938-939).":["u182"],"copy|Risk association is not causation.":["u183"],"copy|The September 2024 alternatives contrasted suggested higher risk with manganese exposure against inverse associations with smoking and caffeinated coffee.":["u184"],"copy|PD is more common in men.":["u185"],"copy|The official locator is p936; the environmental discussion continues on p937.":["u186"],"copy|Hazzard does not present the inverse associations as preventive treatments (p936-937).":["u187"],"copy|PSP, MSA and CBS together have a prevalence roughly 5% to 10% of PD prevalence, and DLB is more common than those other atypical syndromes.":["u188"],"copy|This comparison does not establish the fraction of all parkinsonism that is atypical.":["u189"],"copy|September 2024 also asked about contact sports.":["u190"],"copy|Levodopa crosses the blood-brain barrier as a dopamine precursor.":["u192"],"copy|Carbidopa or benserazide reduces peripheral conversion, improving availability and limiting gastrointestinal effects.":["u193"],"copy|In an older person whose motor symptoms impair quality of life, its greater motor benefit and lower propensity for hallucinations than agonists matter.":["u194"],"copy|Pramipexole and ropinirole can cause daytime somnolence or sudden sleep attacks, ankle edema and impulsive eating, spending, gambling or sexual behavior.":["u195"],"copy|Ask patients and families about new behaviors because the patient may never volunteer them.":["u196"],"copy|Their temporal relation to an agonist supports reviewing and reducing its dose as tolerated (p943-946).":["u197"],"copy|Levodopa versus an agonist.":["u198"],"copy|Clinical trials found fewer dyskinesias and less wearing-off with agonists, but greater neuropsychiatric toxicity, particularly in older adults.":["u199"],"copy|June 2025 selected motor fluctuations as the effect more common with levodopa.":["u200"],"copy|The list prints p943 and p964; the comparison continues on p944, whereas p964 is in the stroke chapter (p943-945).":["u201"],"copy|End of dose versus peak dose.":["u202"],"copy|A useful dose followed by stiffness before the next tablet means the benefit wears off early.":["u203"],"copy|Shortening the interval or adding a COMT inhibitor extends motor coverage.":["u204"],"copy|Entacapone increases effective levodopa exposure and can aggravate dyskinesia.":["u205"],"copy|In contrast, irregular, dance-like movements at peak exposure suggest lowering each dose, sometimes giving smaller doses more often to preserve mobility.":["u206"],"copy|September 2024 omitted timing relative to doses; its reduction answer assumes peak-dose dyskinesia.":["u207"],"copy|Diphasic movements occur as the dose starts and ends and need a different assessment.":["u208"],"copy|June 2022 asked about continuous intestinal levodopa for troublesome fluctuations with a good motor response (p943-946).":["u209"],"copy|Hallucinations versus depression.":["u210"],"copy|Review acute precipitants and other psychoactive medicines.":["u211"],"copy|If hallucinations track dopaminergic treatment, reduce the load as motor function permits.":["u212"],"copy|Abrupt levodopa withdrawal can cause a neuroleptic malignant-like syndrome.":["u213"],"copy|The December 2021 key selected quetiapine for frightening hallucinations; the text also considers pimavanserin, or clozapine with blood-count monitoring when symptoms remain severe or frightening.":["u214"],"copy|Depression may precede motor signs and needs a separate assessment.":["u215"],"copy|A lower dopaminergic dose may help drug-related sleepiness or psychosis but is not a general treatment for depression (p946-947).":["u216"],"copy|Loss of muscle atonia in REM sleep lets a person act out a dream and injure a bed partner.":["u217"],"copy|Ask about the movements and protect the sleeping space.":["u218"],"copy|Clonazepam can suppress the episodes.":["u219"],"copy|Melatonin can be tried first; the chapter describes it as less effective.":["u220"],"copy|Nighttime rigidity from wearing-off has a different mechanism and may improve with better nocturnal motor coverage.":["u221"],"copy|Constipation is among the autonomic features of PD and can worsen with anticholinergic drugs.":["u222"],"copy|June 2022 selected constipation; Hazzard also includes drooling in its nonmotor list (p942, p947-948).":["u223"],"copy|DBS targets the subthalamic nucleus or globus pallidus internus in selected patients with disabling motor fluctuations, dyskinesia or medication-refractory tremor.":["u224"],"copy|A good levodopa response helps identify motor features likely to improve with stimulation, with refractory tremor an exception.":["u225"],"copy|Recurrent falls from postural instability, prominent cognitive impairment or serious depression reduce suitability.":["u226"],"copy|December 2021 selected troublesome on-off states with preserved cognition.":["u227"],"copy|June 2025 selected uncontrolled tremor (p949).":["u228"],"copy|The June 2026 Basic item asked about a dementia medicine in a patient with presyncope and an abnormal ECG.":["u229"],"copy|The list names Chapter 59 but prints p945-946, which describe Parkinson motor fluctuations and medication complications.":["u230"],"copy|The official answer is donepezil.":["u231"],"copy|The link between cholinesterase inhibition and bradycardia appears in the dementia treatment discussion on p912; the printed Parkinson pages do not establish that cardiac adverse effect.":["u232"],"copy|Later-stage care.":["u233"],"copy|Cognitive impairment in PD commonly affects attention and executive function.":["u234"],"copy|Long-established motor disease before dementia favors PDD, whereas dementia around motor onset favors DLB.":["u235"],"copy|Exercise, gait cueing and physical therapy support mobility; swallowing difficulty and loss of independence call for discussions about support, feeding decisions and goals with the patient and caregivers (p939, p942, p948-950).":["u236"],"copy|פרקינסון וגמלת סיעוד בבית.":["u237"],"copy|רעד או ברדיקינזיה אינם כשלעצמם מדד לתלות.":["u238"],"copy|אצל אדם עם פרקינסון בודקים, למשל, אם הוא זקוק לאדם אחר כדי ללכת בתוך הבית, להתלבש, להתרחץ, לאכול או לטפל בהפרשות.":["u239"],"copy|אפשר לעמוד בתנאי התפקודי גם כשנדרשת השגחה צמודה עקב סכנת חיים מיידית או סכנת פגיעה באדם עצמו או בזולת.":["u240"],"copy|הזכאות תלויה גם בגיל הפרישה ובתושבות, בהכנסות, במסגרת המגורים ובכללים בדבר גמלאות אחרות.":["u241"],"copy|הביטוח הלאומי, תנאי הזכאות לגמלת סיעוד, תנאים 1–5.":["u242"],"copy|מה לתעד כאשר התפקוד משתנה לאורך היום.":["u243"],"copy|אם המטופל הולך בכוחות עצמו לאחר נטילת לבודופה אך זקוק לליווי או לעזרה פיזית לפני המנה הבאה, חשוב לתאר באילו שעות ובאילו פעולות נדרשת העזרה.":["u244"],"copy|זו דרך ליישם את הערכת התפקוד במחלה עם תנודות מוטוריות.":["u245"],"copy|הביטוח הלאומי מבקש מסמכים רפואיים ותפקודיים עדכניים, ובכללם מידע על הטיפול התרופתי.":["u246"],"copy|המעריך רשאי להשלים פרטים בטלפון או בביקור בית.":["u247"],"copy|בבדיקה ביתית של אדם עם ירידה קוגניטיבית נדרשת נוכחות בן משפחה או אפוטרופוס.":["u248"],"copy|הביטוח הלאומי, בדיקת יכולת תפקוד, מסלול המעריך המוסמך.":["u249"],"copy|מנגנון התנודות מתואר בפרק זה בעמ׳ 944–945.":["u250"],"copy|הערכה שיקומית לאחר אשפוז.":["u251"],"copy|אם אדם עם פרקינסון הלך לפני מחלה חריפה וכעת זקוק לעזרה בקימה ובהעברות, יש להשוות את תפקודו הנוכחי לתפקוד שקדם לאירוע.":["u252"],"copy|מגבלה קודמת מאפשרת שיקום אם הרופא מעריך שאפשר לשוב לתפקוד הקודם או להשיג שיפור משמעותי.":["u253"],"copy|בירידה קוגניטיבית בודקים אם שיתוף הפעולה מאפשר טיפול.":["u254"],"copy|מומחה בגריאטריה או בשיקום קובע את ההמלצה ואת היעדים בהתייעצות רב־מקצועית.":["u255"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, חוזר מינהל רפואה 4/2009, סעיפים 5.1.4, 5.2 ו־6.1–6.4.":["u256"],"copy|בחירת מסגרת השיקום.":["u257"],"copy|קופת החולים אחראית לשיקום.":["u258"],"copy|לפי הצורך והתנאים בבית ניתן לשקול שיקום בבית, במכון, בשיקום יום או באשפוז.":["u259"],"copy|אצל מטופל שנמצא מתאים לשיקום, צורך בהשגחה רפואית וסיעודית ממושכת או היעדר אפשרות לטיפול מתאים בבית ובקהילה הם התוויות לשיקום באשפוז.":["u260"],"copy|לאחר אשפוז בבית חולים ההמלצה צריכה להופיע במכתב השחרור.":["u261"],"copy|חוזר 4/2009, סעיפים 4.1, 7 ו־10.1.":["u262"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, שיקום גריאטרי, ״ייעוץ רפואי גריאטרי לפני השיקום״ ו״תהליך השיקום הגריאטרי״.":["u263"],"copy|In PDD, established motor parkinsonism begins more than one year before dementia.":["u83"],"copy|Dementia before, together with, or within one year after parkinsonism fits the DLB timing convention (p939, DLB Consortium 2017).":["u83"],"copy|Hazzard advises considering clozapine when quetiapine or pimavanserin is ineffective or hallucinations are frightening. It gives an agranulocytosis risk of 1–2%, directs weekly blood counts, and says to discontinue clozapine if leukopenia develops (pp946–947).Current practice (not the book): for stable normal absolute neutrophil counts, the US Clozaril label specifies weekly ANC in months 1–6, every 2 weeks in months 7–12 and monthly thereafter — US prescribing information, Table 1.":["u35"],"copy|Hazzard describes focused ultrasound as delivering a “radiofrequency ablation to the STN,” with other targets possible, and says it “does not involve anesthesia” (p949, printed wording). The lesion is irreversible and cannot be reprogrammed.Current practice (not the book): the 2018 US device indication specifies unilateral thalamotomy for medication-refractory tremor in tremor-dominant PD, using ultrasound thermal ablation of the ventral intermediate nucleus. Local anesthesia has been used for placement of the stereotactic frame — FDA device indication, 2018, prospective PD thalamotomy study, 2021.":["u40"],"copy|CHAPTER 61 / HAZZARD":["u66"],"copy|At publication, the trial comparing quetiapine with pimavanserin was still in progress (p946).":["u43"],"copy|Exam traps":["u74"],"copy|The 2026 exam tested the diagnosis and treatment of parkinsonism in the following questions, translated from Hebrew.":["u103"],"copy|Pages 938 and 941 describe the syndromes; Chapter 64 links longer exposure to repetitive head impacts with neocortical Lewy body disease and links that pathology with parkinsonism and dementia (p938, p941, p999).":["u191"],"parkinson-disease-and-related-disorders|Parkinson Disease and Related Disorders":["u264"],"recognizing-parkinsonism|Recognizing parkinsonism":["u265"],"the-motor-syndrome|The motor syndrome":["u266"],"frequency-and-susceptibility|Frequency and susceptibility":["u267"],"nigral-cell-loss-and-motor-symptoms|Nigral cell loss and motor symptoms":["u268"],"premotor-and-cognitive-features|Premotor and cognitive features":["u269"],"examining-and-distinguishing-the-syndromes|Examining and distinguishing the syndromes":["u270"],"rest-tremor-bradykinesia-and-rigidity|Rest tremor, bradykinesia and rigidity":["u271"],"what-levodopa-and-dat-imaging-can-tell-you|What levodopa and DaT imaging can tell you":["u272"],"treating-motor-symptoms|Treating motor symptoms":["u273"],"choosing-initial-treatment|Choosing initial treatment":["u274"],"when-benefit-wears-off|When benefit wears off":["u275"],"dyskinesias-at-peak-dose-and-at-its-edges|Dyskinesias at peak dose and at its edges":["u276"],"nonmotor-complications-and-advanced-treatment|Nonmotor complications and advanced treatment":["u277"],"psychosis-and-depression|Psychosis and depression":["u278"],"sleep-autonomic-symptoms-and-cognition|Sleep, autonomic symptoms and cognition":["u279"],"surgery-and-device-assisted-treatment|Surgery and device-assisted treatment":["u280"],"continuing-care|Continuing care":["u281"],"key-numbers|Key numbers":["u282"],"copy|After five years of levodopa, more than 50% develop wearing-off or dyskinesias (p943).":["u283"],"copy|About 50% of people with Parkinson disease develop dementia within 10 years of diagnosis (p942).":["u284"],"test-yourself|Test yourself":["u285"],"copy|First establish whether the motor syndrome is parkinsonism.":["u286"],"copy|Its pattern, timing and response to levodopa guide the diagnosis and subsequent treatment.":["u287"],"copy|Parkinsonism describes a pattern of six motor features: bradykinesia, rest tremor, rigidity, loss of postural reflexes, flexed posture and freezing of gait.":["u288"],"copy|Bradykinesia is central.":["u289"],"copy|Parkinson disease (PD) is the most common neurodegenerative cause of parkinsonism (p935).":["u290"],"copy|A clinical diagnosis can be wrong even when the pattern seems familiar: at autopsy 10% to 20% of clinical diagnoses are not confirmed, while about 20% of older adults without known parkinsonism have midbrain Lewy bodies.":["u291"],"copy|Lewy bodies in the substantia nigra pars compacta are the diagnostic standard at autopsy (p936).":["u292"],"copy|Incidence across all ages is 4.5–19 per 100,000 person-years, rising to 27.2–107.2 per 100,000 person-years after age 60.":["u293"],"copy|Prevalence is 100–200 per 100,000 overall and more than 1 in 100 after age 60 (an estimated 1% to 2%, p935).":["u294"],"copy|PD is about 1.5 times as common in men (p936).":["u295"],"copy|Manganese exposure has been associated with higher risk.":["u296"],"copy|Smoking and caffeinated coffee show inverse associations, which do not establish a preventive treatment (pp936–937).":["u297"],"copy|More than 90% of PD is sporadic, despite the 20 known PARK loci described at publication.":["u298"],"copy|GBA (GBA1) mutations are associated with early-onset PD, particularly in patients of Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry (pp937–938).":["u299"],"copy|PSP, MSA and corticobasal syndrome together have roughly 5% to 10% of the prevalence of PD.":["u300"],"copy|Dementia with Lewy bodies is more common than these other atypical syndromes.":["u301"],"copy|This prevalence ratio does not give the proportion of all parkinsonism that is atypical (pp938, 941).":["u302"],"copy|Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta and misfolded alpha-synuclein in Lewy bodies are characteristic.":["u303"],"copy|Normally dopamine promotes movement through the excitatory D1 direct pathway and limits the inhibitory D2 indirect pathway.":["u304"],"copy|Loss of dopamine shifts the balance toward motor inhibition.":["u305"],"copy|After the third decade, normal aging costs 0.5% to 1% of nigrostriatal terminals per year.":["u306"],"copy|In PD the posterior and dorsal putamen are preferentially affected: an estimated 60% to 80% of posterior-putaminal terminals are lost by motor onset (p938).":["u307"],"copy|In the six-stage Braak model, olfactory, sleep and autonomic changes can precede the motor syndrome in stages 1–2.":["u308"],"copy|Motor symptoms emerge with nigral involvement at stage 3.":["u309"],"copy|Cortical Lewy bodies and cognitive decline occur in stages 5–6 (p939).":["u310"],"copy|Longer exposure to repetitive head impacts in contact sports has also been associated with neocortical Lewy body disease and with parkinsonism and dementia (Chapter 64, p999).":["u311"],"copy|Look first for slowness and loss of amplitude with repeated movement, often with gradual asymmetric onset.":["u312"],"copy|A supported limb trembles at rest in typical PD.":["u313"],"copy|Tremor while holding out the arms, writing or using a spoon suggests essential tremor, although mixed tremor and coexistence are possible.":["u314"],"copy|Bilateral hand and head tremor that improves after alcohol fits the latter pattern.":["u315"],"copy|A beta blocker was the accepted treatment in the December 2021 case; propranolol has evidence for limb tremor (p940; AAN clinician summary, 2011).":["u316"],"copy|The 2015 MDS criteria for clinically established PD require bradykinesia, no exclusion criterion and at least two of four supportive findings: a levodopa response, levodopa-induced dyskinesia, rest tremor, and olfactory loss or cardiac sympathetic denervation.":["u317"],"copy|A rest tremor beginning in one hand supports PD; it is not a prerequisite.":["u318"],"copy|Impaired postural reflexes can accompany bradykinesia later.":["u319"],"copy|Early prominent postural instability, symmetric onset, an absent rest tremor or a poor levodopa response should prompt consideration of an alternative (pp939–941, Tables 61-3 and 61-4).":["u320"],"copy|Bradykinesia, limb rigidity and distal dexterity usually respond best to levodopa.":["u321"],"copy|Axial symptoms such as hypophonia, dysphagia and postural instability often persist because they also reflect nondopaminergic injury (p939).":["u322"],"copy|DaT SPECT demonstrates nigrostriatal terminal loss.":["u323"],"copy|Essential tremor normally preserves uptake; DaT SPECT is approved to distinguish it from PD in atypical tremor and can help distinguish drug-induced parkinsonism.":["u324"],"copy|PSP, MSA and DLB can all reduce uptake.":["u325"],"copy|An abnormal scan therefore cannot choose among them (pp940–941).":["u326"],"copy|Severe early orthostatic hypotension, bladder or erectile dysfunction, cerebellar signs and nocturnal stridor suggest MSA.":["u327"],"copy|Median onset is in the sixth decade and mean time from diagnosis to severe disability is about five years (pp942–943).":["u328"],"copy|Early backward falls and loss of downward gaze favor PSP.":["u329"],"copy|Asymmetric rigidity or dystonia with limb myoclonus, apraxia, cortical sensory loss or an alien limb favors corticobasal syndrome (Table 61-3, p940; Table 61-4, p941).":["u330"],"copy|Levodopa crosses the blood–brain barrier.":["u331"],"copy|Carbidopa (Sinemet in combination) or benserazide (Madopar in combination) limits peripheral conversion, increases availability and reduces gastrointestinal adverse effects.":["u332"],"copy|Levodopa gives the strongest motor benefit when symptoms impair quality of life.":["u333"],"copy|In trials, dopamine agonists caused fewer dyskinesias and less wearing-off than levodopa.":["u334"],"copy|Hallucinations, confusion and psychosis were more frequent with agonists, particularly in older adults; levodopa is generally safer after age 70 (p944).":["u335"],"copy|Pramipexole and ropinirole may cause sudden sleep attacks, daytime somnolence, ankle edema and impulsive eating, spending, gambling or sexual behavior.":["u336"],"copy|Ask the family as well as the patient and consider reducing the agonist as tolerated if the timing fits.":["u337"],"copy|Ergot agonists—pergolide, bromocriptine and cabergoline—can cause valvular, retroperitoneal or pleuropulmonary fibrosis; pergolide was withdrawn from the US market.":["u338"],"copy|Transdermal rotigotine can help when swallowing or nocturnal akinesia is a problem.":["u339"],"copy|Subcutaneous apomorphine or the sublingual film (Kynmobi) can rapidly rescue an off state; apomorphine may be the most powerful agonist and can cause intense nausea (pp943–946).":["u340"],"copy|With wearing-off, a useful levodopa dose fails to control symptoms for six hours.":["u341"],"copy|Shorten the interval or add a COMT inhibitor.":["u342"],"copy|Entacapone 200 mg with each levodopa dose increases effective exposure and may require a lower levodopa dose to avoid dyskinesia.":["u343"],"copy|Tolcapone comes in 100- and 200-mg tablets; the printed account attributes US market withdrawal to hepatotoxicity (p944).":["u344"],"copy|Opicapone is given once daily.":["u345"],"copy|Once-daily istradefylline, an A2A antagonist, also reduces off time with carbidopa/levodopa in patients with fluctuations.":["u346"],"copy|Selegiline, rasagiline and safinamide (MAO-B inhibitors) offer mild symptomatic benefit, including a small benefit for mild wearing-off, without proven neuroprotection.":["u347"],"copy|Domperidone acts peripherally against gastrointestinal upset from levodopa or agonists and is unavailable in the US (Table 61-5, p944; p945).":["u348"],"copy|Continuous intestinal levodopa is an option for troublesome fluctuations when a good motor response remains (pp943–946).":["u349"],"copy|Irregular, dance-like movements at peak exposure favor smaller individual levodopa doses, sometimes more often to maintain mobility.":["u350"],"copy|Diphasic dyskinesias instead occur as a dose starts and ends; mixed chorea and dystonia predominantly affect the legs (Table 61-6, pp945–946).":["u351"],"copy|Confirm the timing before reducing individual doses.":["u352"],"copy|Amantadine blocks NMDA receptors.":["u353"],"copy|Immediate-release amantadine is described at 100 mg twice daily for motor symptoms, with higher doses for dyskinesia (p944); 100–200 mg twice daily is described for selected patients with peak-dose dyskinesia (p946).":["u354"],"copy|Current practice: amantadine and renal function.":["u355"],"copy|Reduced renal clearance in older adults can cause accumulation.":["u356"],"copy|Adjust immediate-release dosing to renal function, age and neuropsychiatric tolerability; monitor confusion, hallucinations, edema and livedo reticularis.":["u357"],"copy|The 300–400 mg/day range is not a routine geriatric starting dose (US prescribing information).":["u358"],"copy|Buspirone up to 20 mg/day is another option described for selected patients with dyskinesia (p946).":["u359"],"copy|Look for acute illness and other psychoactive drugs first.":["u360"],"copy|If hallucinations track dopaminergic treatment, reduce the burden as motor function allows.":["u361"],"copy|Withdraw antiparkinsonian drugs stepwise over about three days when severe psychosis requires withdrawal: abruptly stopping levodopa can cause a neuroleptic-malignant-like syndrome (p946).":["u362"],"copy|Depression may precede motor symptoms and needs its own assessment; lowering dopaminergic treatment can improve medication-related sleepiness or psychosis without treating depression (pp946–947).":["u363"],"copy|Table 61-7 lists clozapine and quetiapine.":["u364"],"copy|The text adds pimavanserin, which has prospective trial data in PD psychosis; the head-to-head comparison with quetiapine was still underway at publication (pp946–947).":["u365"],"copy|If symptoms persist, the printed options include quetiapine starting at 25 mg at bedtime or pimavanserin 17 mg once or twice daily, with a lower dose for CYP3A4 inhibitors (p946).":["u366"],"copy|Current practice: pimavanserin dose.":["u367"],"copy|Pimavanserin 34 mg PO once daily, no titration; 10 mg once daily with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors — FDA Nuplazid label, 2026.":["u368"],"copy|Clozapine may be considered if quetiapine or pimavanserin is ineffective or hallucinations are frightening.":["u369"],"copy|The printed regimen specifies weekly blood counts, an agranulocytosis risk of 1% to 2%, and discontinuation if leukopenia develops (pp946–947).":["u370"],"copy|Current practice: clozapine blood counts.":["u371"],"copy|For stable normal absolute neutrophil counts, the US Clozaril label specifies weekly ANC in months 1–6, every 2 weeks in months 7–12 and monthly thereafter — US prescribing information, Table 1.":["u372"],"copy|Loss of REM atonia permits dream enactment and injury to a bed partner.":["u373"],"copy|Clonazepam 0.5–2 mg at bedtime can suppress episodes; melatonin is a less effective option that may be tried first.":["u374"],"copy|Nighttime rigidity from wearing-off may instead respond to longer nocturnal motor coverage.":["u375"],"copy|Anticholinergic drugs can worsen constipation, and drooling is another nonmotor feature (pp942, 947–948).":["u376"],"copy|Attention and executive function are often impaired.":["u377"],"copy|Parkinson disease dementia (PDD) follows established motor parkinsonism by more than one year; dementia preceding, accompanying or beginning within one year of motor onset favors DLB (p939; DLB Consortium, 2017).":["u378"],"copy|Presyncope with an abnormal ECG in a patient taking donepezil should prompt consideration of bradycardia from cholinesterase inhibition (Chapter 59, p912).":["u379"],"copy|Distinguish it from adverse effects and motor fluctuations of antiparkinsonian drugs (pp945–946).":["u380"],"copy|Deep brain stimulation of the subthalamic nucleus or globus pallidus internus was FDA-approved in 2002.":["u381"],"copy|Consider it for disabling fluctuations, dyskinesia or medication-refractory tremor.":["u382"],"copy|A good levodopa response helps predict which motor features improve, with refractory tremor an exception.":["u383"],"copy|Recurrent falls from postural instability, substantial cognitive impairment or serious depression reduce suitability.":["u384"],"copy|Postural instability and other axial deficits do not improve reliably (p949).":["u385"],"copy|Current practice: infusion when apomorphine or DBS cannot control symptoms.":["u386"],"copy|NICE recommends subcutaneous foslevodopa/foscarbidopa infusion as an option for adults with advanced levodopa-responsive Parkinson disease, severe motor fluctuations and hyperkinesia or dyskinesia despite available medicines, only when apomorphine or DBS cannot be used or no longer controls symptoms and the commercial arrangement applies; FDA approved it on 16 October 2024 for motor fluctuations in adults with advanced Parkinson disease - NICE TA934 2023, 1.1 p4; FDA NDA216962 approval package cover and approval letter p1":["u387"],"copy|Focused ultrasound produces an irreversible lesion that cannot be reprogrammed.":["u388"],"copy|The printed description uses “radiofrequency ablation to the STN,” mentions other possible targets and says the procedure “does not involve anesthesia” (p949).":["u389"],"copy|Current practice: focused ultrasound target and anesthesia.":["u390"],"copy|The 2018 US device indication specifies unilateral thalamotomy for medication-refractory tremor in tremor-dominant PD, using ultrasound thermal ablation of the ventral intermediate nucleus.":["u391"],"copy|Local anesthesia has been used for placement of the stereotactic frame — FDA device indication, 2018, prospective PD thalamotomy study, 2021.":["u392"],"copy|Pitfall.":["u393"],"copy|Table 61-7 lists antimuscarinics for tremor.":["u394"],"copy|Avoid trihexyphenidyl and benztropine after age 70 because of cognitive impairment, hallucinations and an association with freezing of gait (pp946–947).":["u395"],"copy|Exercise, gait cueing and physical therapy support mobility.":["u396"],"copy|Ask about falls, swallowing and the tasks for which help is needed.":["u397"],"copy|Discuss fee